WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:09.750
SPEAKER: As a
developer today, you

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.970
can launch solutions
faster than ever,

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.190
building on the
capabilities of Google Cloud

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.570
platform, your custom
services, and third-party APIs.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.060
But as more and more
services are integrated,

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.730
your solutions can increase
the complexity, becoming

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.850
hard to understand, and even
harder to develop, debug,

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.470
and visualize.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.950
You could write custom services
to manage these integrations

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.900
and host them in
manually-maintained virtual

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.260
machines or link them together
with a carefully crafted

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.210
event-driven architecture.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.280
Even with event-driven
architecture,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.950
observing the progress
of a workflow execution

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.530
is nearly impossible,
especially when

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:50.490
a workflow might run for
hours, days, or even months.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.590
Implementing error
handling can be difficult.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.600
When something fails
along the chain of events,

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.150
it becomes burdensome
to track down.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.250
Plus, chaining services
together gives you

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.880
a growing distributed system
with less predictable behavior

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.260
over time.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.830
Changes to one workflow
may break another workflow

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.930
also using those events.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.780
Without a central definition
of the entire workflow,

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.820
it can be hard to
see the full picture

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.350
and make appropriate changes.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:20.250
So how can you best use the
power of different services

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.520
in your custom workflow
without all of these issues?

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.400
By using Google Cloud Workflows.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.920
Cloud Workflows is a
fully managed service

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.770
that gives you the power
to integrate microservices

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:35.250
and APIs while also error
handling and streamlining

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.810
future development.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.300
From one central
place, you can describe

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.180
steps that combine custom
services, Google Cloud

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:46.020
products, and anything
with HTTP APIs

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.400
into an automated workflow.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.610
And because it's
serverless, workflow scales

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:54.640
to meet your demand, so you
only pay for what you use.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:56.490
So how does it work?

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.050
Let's look at the
two primary use

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.680
cases, event-driven
workflows and batch jobs.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.790
Event-driven workflows execute
when a certain trigger happens.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.370
For example, let's say one
of your e-commerce customers

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.370
has just placed an order,
and they need their invoice.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.400
A new e-commerce event order
kicks off the workflow.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.820
First up, a cloud or an app
receives data from the event

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.440
and generates a PDF.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.090
Then, the PDF is
passed to a cloud

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.820
function that encrypts and
compresses it into a ZIP file.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:28.050
And finally, the PDF is sent
to a third-party email API,

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.570
sending the invoice
to the customer.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:34.470
Batch workflows consist of
running large jobs potentially

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.540
on a regular basis.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.690
For example, a nightly job to
check on any incomplete orders

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.280
from your e-commerce site.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.280
Each night at midnight,
the workflow is kicked off.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.740
The database is queried
for any incomplete orders

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:48.570
in the past 24 hours.

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.030
Each valid account is sent
to a cloud function that can

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.100
generate a personalized offer.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:56.460
The offer is sent to a separate
function to generate an email.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.330
And those emails are sent
using the same third-party API.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:03.450
In both cases, Workflow
gives you a holistic approach

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:04.830
to workflow management.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:07.800
From the user interface or
from your favorite editor,

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.020
you can build custom
workflows and investigate

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.820
ones that are already running.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.070
When calling other
Google Cloud services,

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.860
Workflow handles all
the underlying details,

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:18.960
like transport
authentication and waiting

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.030
for long-running operations.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.400
Google Cloud IAM
makes it even easier

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.440
to manage permissions so
you don't need to worry

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.110
about unauthenticated requests.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.090
Workflow also gives
you full visibility

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:31.210
into what's happening.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:34.560
You can see each step execute
as your workflow handles errors,

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:37.500
retries, and recovers
from failures, even

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.060
for third-party APIs.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.960
Plus, Workflow executes with
low latency and no cold starts

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.970
with fully-managed
scaling, making them

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.520
ideal for event-driven tasks.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.400
If your workflow is a batch
orchestration workflow

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.890
for data engineering
or ETL, you may

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.930
want to look at Google Cloud
Composer, built on a Apache

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.800
Airflow with data analysis
focus integration.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.590
It's built for workflows
involving data analysis.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:03.150
On the other hand,
if your workflow

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.240
is focused on orchestrating
work done by services,

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.230
Cloud Workflow delivers a fully
managed, easy-to-use solution.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:11.860
Bring your
mission-critical workflows

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:14.550
to Google Cloud Workflows
to discover how quickly you

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:18.000
can deploy powerful solutions,
while knowing everything

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.640
will scale operate reliably
in a fully managed,

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.900
cost-effective environment.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:25.320
Check out
cloud.google.com/workflows

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.420
to learn more and to
try it out yourself.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:30.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

